{"text":"Codex approach gate: ran one shot; the companion returned an empty payload (no approaches). Per the gate's rule an incomplete call degrades — noted once, no retry — so the approaches below are mine alone, with no independent Codex input to fold in.\n\n## Short design\n\n**Recommendation on your question: word boundary, with a hard cut as fallback.** The reason is that the failure mode differs in kind. Exact-length truncation is never wrong, just ugly — it produces `\"the quick bro...\"`. Word-boundary truncation is prettier in the common case and degrades to exactly the exact-length behavior whenever there's no space to break on, so it's a strict superset: you pay ~4 lines and one `lastIndexOf` for it, and you can never do worse than the simple version. The one case where it looks bad is a short first word followed by a very long one — `\"I antidisestablishmentarianism\"` at 20 becomes `\"I...\"` — which I'd accept rather than add a minimum-retention guard for.\n\n**Behavior**\n\n- New option `truncate: <number>` — the max length. One behavior, no second flag to pick the strategy.\n- `maxLength` is a hard cap including the `'...'`, per your answer: budget is `maxLength - 3` characters of text, then the ellipsis.\n- Cut at `budget`, then back up to the last space if there is one; trim trailing whitespace so you get `\"hello...\"` not `\"hello ...\"`. No space in range → keep the hard cut.\n- `maxLength <= 3` → return `'...'.slice(0, maxLength)`. `truncate: 0` is falsy and so is a no-op, consistent"}
